I have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Summary

This is the **Spring PetClinic REST** sample (Spring Boot, Maven). The "member/customer" domain is modeled on the **`Owner`** entity. There is currently **no `membershipNumber` field anywhere in `src/main`** — it exists only as an expectation in an acceptance test. The `customerCode` and `registrationDate` fields already exist. The established pattern for a derived, read-only field like `membershipNumber` is a `@Transient` getter on the entity that MapStruct auto-maps into the generated DTO.

---

## 1. Entity/model class with the fields

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`** (extends `Person`)

```java
@Column(name = "registration_date")
private LocalDate registrationDate;      // lines 54-55

@Column(name = "customer_code")
private String customerCode;             // lines 57-58

@Column(name = "household_id")
private String householdId;              // lines 60-61

@Column(name = "namesake_count")
private Integer namesakeCount;           // lines 63-64
```
With standard getters/setters (lines 101-131). There is **no `membershipNumber` field** — it would need to be added here.

The superclass **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java`** holds `firstName`/`lastName` and — importantly — shows the **derived-field pattern** you'd reuse for `membershipNumber`:

```java
@Transient
public String getDisplayName() {          // lines 59-62
    return this.lastName + ", " + this.firstName;
}

@Transient
public String getInitials() {             // lines 68-72
    return Character.toUpperCase(this.firstName.charAt(0)) + "."
        + Character.toUpperCase(this.lastName.charAt(0)) + ".";
}
```
These `@Transient` computed getters are not DB columns; MapStruct picks them up automatically because the DTO declares matching read-only properties. `membershipNumber` (= `customerCode + "-M" + YY`) fits this exact mold and would most naturally be a `@Transient getMembershipNumber()` on `Owner` (it needs `customerCode` + `registrationDate`, both on `Owner`).

---

## 2. Service layer — where owners are created / codes assigned

Creation is orchestrated in the REST controller, **not** a single service method. The service layer just persists.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** — `addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`, lines 136-160. The assignment pipeline (lines 149-155):

```java
if (duplicate) {
    this.householdAssigner.assign(owner);
}
this.registrationDateDefaulter.applyDefault(owner);  // sets registrationDate
this.customerCodeAssigner.assign(owner);             // sets customerCode
this.namesakeCounter.assign(owner);                  // sets namesakeCount
this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
```
This is where a `membershipNumber` would be assigned **if** it were stored — but given the derived-getter pattern, it more likely needs no line here at all. Note there is also a **V2** controller under `.../rest/controller/v2/`.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`**:
- `saveOwner(Owner)` — lines 232-237 (`ownerRepository.save`)
- `countOwners()` — lines 245-249 (used by the code sequence)
- `findAllOwners()` — lines 125-129
- Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java` (lines 57-62)

---

## 3. Existing tests related to membership number / customer code / registration

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** — this is the spec you must satisfy:

```java
/** membership-number: 'membershipNumber' = '<customerCode>-M<YY>', YY = last two
 * digits of the registrationDate year. Derived from the owner's own fields, so seed-independent. */
@Test
void coreMembershipNumberFormat() throws Exception {
    int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
    JsonNode o = fetchOwner(id);
    String yy = o.get("registrationDate").asText().substring(2, 4);
    assertEquals(o.get("customerCode").asText() + "-M" + yy, o.get("membershipNumber").asText());
}
```
So the format is `<customerCode>-M<YY>`, where `YY` = characters at index 2-4 of the ISO `registrationDate` (the 2-digit year). Example: customerCode `SUR-0007`, registered 2026 → `SUR-0007-M26`.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`** — black-box test harness. Key facts: `@Transactional` rollback + `@WithMockUser(roles OWNER_ADMIN...)`; tests hit only `POST /api/owners` and `GET /api/owners/{id}`; `ownerNode()` (lines 117-125) builds a valid payload with **no** `registrationDate` (so it defaults to today), meaning `membershipNumber` must reflect the current year.

No unit tests exist for `CustomerCodeAssigner`/`RegistrationDateDefaulter` themselves.

---

## 4. Similar formatting/assignment logic to reuse as a pattern

Two relevant patterns, both under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/`:

**a) Derived read-only field (BEST match for `membershipNumber`)** — `Person.getDisplayName()` / `getInitials()` (`@Transient` getters, auto-mapped to DTO via MapStruct). This is seed-independent and computed from the owner's own fields, exactly as the test comment demands.

**b) `CustomerCodeAssigner.java`** — the closest string-formatting analogue (lines 47-52):
```java
public void assign(Owner owner) {
    String lastName = owner.getLastName();
    String prefix = lastName.substring(0, Math.min(3, lastName.length())).toUpperCase();
    long sequence = clinicService.countOwners() + 1;
    owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%04d", prefix, sequence));
}
```
Sibling components with the same `@Component` + `assign(Owner)` / `applyDefault(Owner)` shape: `RegistrationDateDefaulter.java` (defaults `registrationDate` to `LocalDate.now(clock)`), `NamesakeCounter.java`, `HouseholdAssigner.java`, `HouseholdDuplicateChecker.java`.

**Important — DTO is generated, not hand-written.** `OwnerDto`/`OwnerFieldsDto` are generated by the **openapi-generator** Maven plugin from **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`**. The `Owner` schema there (lines 2070-2135) declares `customerCode`, `householdId`, `displayName`, `initials`, `namesakeCount` as `readOnly` — but **`membershipNumber` is NOT present**. To surface it you must add a `readOnly` `membershipNumber` property to that `Owner` schema (mirroring `customerCode` at lines 2085-2093), then add the derived `@Transient getMembershipNumber()` on `Owner`. MapStruct (`OwnerMapper.java`, `toOwnerDto`) will map it automatically — no explicit `@Mapping` needed, as with `displayName`/`initials`.

Relevant files:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (OwnerFields lines 1991-2069; Owner lines 2070-2135)
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`

---

## 5. Build tool and running tests

**Maven** (wrapper committed). `pom.xml` uses `spring-boot-starter-parent`, `mapstruct` 1.6.3, `openapi-generator` plugin 7.23.0.

- Run all tests: `./mvnw test`
- Run just the acceptance test: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`
- Full build: `./mvnw clean install` (readme.md line 218)
- Run the app: `./mvnw spring-boot:run` (readme.md line 25)

Note: the DTOs are code-generated during the Maven build from `openapi.yml`, so any schema change requires a rebuild (`./mvnw ...`) before tests see the new `membershipNumber` field.

I made no modifications.

